### 1351 — Pre-existing drop-summary flake makes full-package runs ~11% unreliable (review BUG-260720-2p4fln)
- FINDING: `PacketFlowBridgeFaultTests.swift:426` ("drop summaries are window-limited and flush unsummarized drops at stop") intermittently observes 0 `packet_bridge.drop_summary` log messages where 1 is expected. Reviewer measured 6 failures in ~50 full `swift test` runs on the BUG-2p4fln tree and 4/30 on clean base `0d6836d` — pre-existing, not introduced by BUG-2p4fln.
- ROOT CAUSE: Likely explains the unresolved 1256 anomaly (105-test era, same symptom class); that entry's "monitoring" status is now answered: the flake is real, frequent, and in the fault-injection suite delivered by TASK-260715-3dn813.
- STATUS: Out of BUG-2p4fln scope (bridge counters/tests explicitly excluded). Needs its own bug: `make validate-core` currently fails ~1 in 8 runs for unrelated work. Evidence logs: `.temp/BUG-260720-2p4fln/review/full-run-*.log`.

### 1349 — HEV 100-cycle descriptor-baseline check races cross-suite parallelism (review BUG-260720-2p4fln)
- REGRESSION: New `HEVBridgeIntegrationTests.swift:275` final check `eventually(openDescriptorCount() == baseline)` failed 2 times in ~93 full-package runs (first reviewer `make validate-core` run included); 0 failures in 45+ filtered `--filter HEVIntegrationTests` runs across implementer and reviewer.
- ROOT CAUSE: `openDescriptorCount()` counts process-global descriptors, but Swift Testing runs suites in parallel; flake log `.temp/BUG-260720-2p4fln/review/hev-flake-85.log` shows the fuzz suite still live when the 100-cycle test captured its baseline. Any concurrent suite's descriptor delta between baseline capture and final check breaks exact equality permanently; the 5s poll then times out. `.serialized` only guards within the HEV suite. Same fragile pattern at `HEVBridgeIntegrationTests.swift:230` (fault test, 3s).
- DECISION: Review verdict — product changes accepted on evidence (stats snapshot, single boundary stop, quit-safety, TSan, lint, boundaries all green); task routed to-dev only to make the two descriptor-baseline assertions isolation-safe under full-package parallel runs and re-demonstrate repeatability.

### 1339 — HEV statistics and teardown lifecycle repaired without changing upstream (BUG-260720-2p4fln)
- FIX: `HEVDescriptorBorrowHandle` snapshots all four HEV traffic counters immediately before an intentional quit and publishes that snapshot after join, before releasing the process lease. The spontaneous-return guard remains intact: Swift never calls upstream quit after main has returned, and endpoint B remains borrower-owned until the bridge closes it after join.
- ROOT CAUSE / DECISION: `boundary.stop()` was a product double invocation (`requestStop()` plus `waitForReturn()`), not a bad integration expectation. A lock-coalesced stop task now invokes the boundary once and lets both lifecycle paths await the same operation; unit and real-HEV cycle tests assert one start/one stop.
- TEST DISCIPLINE: The lwIP `tcp_slowtmr: active pcb->state != CLOSED` assertion came from tearing HEV down while test-owned SOCKS channels or a half-open TCP fault PCB remained live. Integration tests now close transferred channels before bridge stop and exercise reverse-write failure with UDP-in-TCP, which proves the same bridge fatal path without leaving a synthetic TCP handshake half-open across HEV runs.
- EXPECTATION FIX: A stalled SOCKS peer does not guarantee Darwin endpoint-B backpressure because native HEV may continue draining the tunnel descriptor. The stall test now asserts the deterministic contract (`sent + would-block drops + ENOBUFS drops == received`) and allows the legitimate zero-drop schedule instead of forcing timing-dependent loss.
- VERIFICATION: Five consecutive full HEV runs passed (13/13 each), the full package and `make validate-core` passed 110/110, the exact HEV suite passed under ThreadSanitizer, strict Swift format and `git diff --check` were clean, and the pinned HEV XCFramework was unchanged. Evidence: `.temp/BUG-260720-2p4fln/`.

### 1320 — Real HEV traffic counters are cleared before Swift observes them (TASK-260715-35wctc)
- STOP-THE-LINE: The new real PacketFlowBridge → pinned HEV/lwIP → authenticated SOCKS integration test completes concurrent IPv4 and IPv6 TCP handshakes, bidirectional payloads, IPv4 half-close, and IPv6 reset, but both published post-stop HEV packet gauges are zero.
- ROOT CAUSE: `HEVDescriptorBorrowHandle.waitForReturn()` joins HEV and only then calls `runtime.statistics()` (`Sources/ReluxTunnelNativeAdapter/HEVIntegration.swift:409-423`). Pinned unmodified HEV calls `hev_socks5_tunnel_fini()` before `hev_socks5_tunnel_main_from_str()` returns, and that fini resets all four `stat_tx_*`/`stat_rx_*` globals to zero (`src/hev-socks5-tunnel.c:657-671`). The public stats call after join therefore cannot observe completed-run traffic.
- EVIDENCE: `swift test --filter realHEVTCPMatrix` reaches the real SOCKS fixture and validates the full stream lifecycle, then fails only the `hev_transmitted_packets > 0` and `hev_received_packets > 0` assertions. Log: `.temp/TASK-260715-35wctc/swift-test-tcp-01.log`.
- OPTIONS: Preserve unmodified HEV by capturing statistics while main is still active (immediately before quit, plus an explicit strategy for spontaneous return), add a wrapper-owned live snapshot to the native runtime seam, or explicitly remove completed-run HEV counters from the acceptance contract. Recommendation: retain the counters and revise the runtime seam to snapshot before HEV fini; do not weaken the integration assertions or patch the pinned HEV binary.

