### 1208 — libssh2 adapter hits third-round channel/exec/window API boundary (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
- BLOCKER: Pinned `ssh2_channel_read` automatically sends WINDOW_ADJUST before returning consumer bytes when remaining credit crosses its private threshold. There is no public manual-window mode, so consumer-earned credit and the immutable advertised cap cannot be enforced.
- BLOCKER: `ssh2_channel_open` parses all RFC 4254 rejection reasons, then collapses them to `LIBSSH2_ERROR_CHANNEL_FAILURE`; no public typed/numeric reason survives. The remaining diagnostic string is not a stable control-flow API.
- BLOCKER: Channels store only integer `exit_status` defaulting to zero and an optional signal name. The public API cannot distinguish explicit status 0 from no exit metadata and discards the RFC `core dumped` signal bit.
- ROUTING: This is the third fork-gap round anticipated by TASK-260720-2sltje. Do not add a partial/mock-only adapter. Recommend failing libssh2 red at M0 unless its value justifies one explicitly approved consolidated fork extension for manual windows, typed open failures, and lossless exec-exit metadata; the alternative is an explicit M0-to-M3/common-contract revision. Exact evidence and resume decision are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_third-public-api-blocker.md`.

### 1200 — Bounded libssh2 observation fork clears the remaining public-API clauses (TASK-260720-2sltje)
- DECISION: The existing libssh2 patch remains a single allowlisted six-file delta. It adds typed server-initiated KEX `STARTED`/`SUCCEEDED`/`FAILED` observation with a monotonic generation and one bounded, nonblocking reply-correlated global-request operation; packet ordering, encryption, MAC, KEX algorithms, and crypto implementations remain unchanged.
- DECISION: A timed-out global request stays outstanding until its late reply is drained. This deliberately prevents an unnumbered SSH `REQUEST_SUCCESS`/`REQUEST_FAILURE` from being miscorrelated with a newer request; callers can record a miss at `LIBSSH2_ERROR_TIMEOUT` and continue the same operation to restore the request slot.
- VERIFICATION: Two clean source rebuilds produced byte-identical XCFramework locks; checksum/allowlist/tamper, public-symbol, notice, static/extension-safe, strict C warning, Swift packaging, 64-test core, and `swift build` gates pass. The local OpenSSH fixture observed 14 server-KEX transitions, including a deliberate failure, plus measured reply RTT and a deterministic timeout/miss.
- ANOMALY: The broader pre-existing HEV Apple matrix stops before any Xcode build because `HevSocks5Tunnel.xcframework` contains macOS 10.14 and 11.0 object minima while its manifest requires only 10.14. This artifact is outside the libssh2 delta; the task-specific XCFramework inspection and required `make validate-core` gates pass.

### 1145 — libssh2 adapter remains blocked on server-rekey observability and keepalive replies (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
- BLOCKER: The accepted `libssh2_session_rekey` fork clears client byte/time/manual trigger initiation only. The exact public header exports no KEX lifecycle callback or generation query. Pinned libssh2 handles inbound `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` entirely inside private `packet.c`/`transport.c` state, so the adapter cannot truthfully emit `.serverInitiated`, mark `activeKeyExchange`, increment generation, or distinguish server-KEX success/failure through the public boundary.
- BLOCKER: `libssh2_keepalive_send` writes `keepalive@libssh2.org` with `want_reply=1` but returns after queueing and stores no outstanding request or reply outcome. The public API exposes no generic reply-requiring global request or request-success/failure correlation. Consequently the adapter cannot measure reply RTT, enforce reply timeout/miss allowance, or distinguish acknowledgement from silence.
- EVIDENCE: The public macOS XCFramework exports only `libssh2_session_rekey`, `libssh2_keepalive_config`, and `libssh2_keepalive_send` among relevant symbols. `keepalive.c:52-96` contains no reply wait/state; inbound server rekey is private at `packet.c:1336-1369` and `transport.c:407-425,1039-1048`. Trace callbacks are unavailable in non-debug builds and are not a supported protocol API.
- ROUTING: Do not report these gates green or build a mock-only partial adapter. Recommend a separately reviewed minimal fork extension exposing typed server-KEX lifecycle plus a nonblocking reply-correlated global-request/keepalive API; alternatives are explicitly failing the libssh2 candidate or revising the mandatory contract. Exact options and resume decision are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_server-rekey-keepalive-blocker.md`.

### 1115 — Minimal libssh2 public client-rekey fork clears the adapter blocker (TASK-260720-3vwls7)
- DECISION: Pinned libssh2 `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` carries one allowlisted two-file patch: `include/libssh2.h` publicly declares `libssh2_session_rekey`, and `src/session.c` adds a null-checked, nonblocking-friendly `BLOCK_ADJUST` wrapper over the existing `ssh2_kex_exchange(session, 1, &session->startup_key_state)`. No crypto, algorithm, packet, channel, or private-header surface changed.
- SUPPLY CHAIN: The builder verifies libssh2 and OpenSSL 3.5.7 archive SHA-256 values before extraction or patch application, checks source-license hashes, rebuilds five static Apple architecture slices, emits the combined BSD-3-Clause/Apache-2.0/acknowledgement notice, and fails closed on patch/hash/path drift. Two clean rebuilds produced byte-identical XCFramework artifact locks.
- VERIFICATION: The public-header-only C integration test drove the wrapper through three `EAGAIN` results, observed client-initiated NEWKEYS installation, then forced OpenSSH `RekeyLimit` server-initiated NEWKEYS and proved post-rekey channels remained usable. Fork verify/rekey/source-tamper gates, Swift public-module packaging test, native seam tests, strict Swift format, 62 core tests, and `swift build` pass; `ReluxTunnelCore` remains free of libssh2/OpenSSL imports.

### 1030 — libssh2 adapter stops on absent public client-rekey API (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
- BLOCKER: Exact pinned archive `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` reproduced SHA-256 `744ba3e9…c87c`. Public `include/libssh2.h` has no rekey operation; initial `libssh2_session_handshake` calls private `ssh2_kex_exchange(..., reexchange=0)`, while only inbound `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` reaches private `ssh2_kex_exchange(..., reexchange=1)` from `src/packet.c`.
- IMPACT: Mandatory byte/time/explicit client rekey and `SSHTransport.requestRekey` cannot be implemented through the pinned public API. Calling the private symbol, exporting it in-task, replaying handshake, or reporting success without KEX would violate the task's no-patch/no-hidden-fallback boundary. Product code and partial packaging stopped before implementation.
- ROUTING: Recommend recording libssh2 red/failed at this pin. The alternative is a separately authorized, audited libssh2 fork/public rekey hook (or a future upstream pin followed by full re-audit). Exact evidence and tradeoffs are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_libssh2-rekey-blocker.md`.

### 1020 — ReluxNIOSSH adapter blocked by TCP ownership and consumer-credit gaps (TASK-260715-1af33i)
- BLOCKER: The neutral network seam injects an `SSHTCPConnection` with readiness/read/write/close only, while `NIOSSHHandler` can run only inside a NIO `Channel`. The seam exposes neither a transferable connected socket nor an engine-bootstrap capability. Opening a second socket ignores injection; adapter-private downcast breaks fake replaceability; a socketpair pump is a forced-fit extra transport.
- BLOCKER: The fork debits on SSH-frame intake but returns the entire frame's credit immediately when `SSHChildChannel` fires it into the adapter pipeline. `SSHByteChannel.read(maximumBytes:)` may consume only a prefix, leaving an adapter-buffered suffix after the fork has already advertised credit. The fork also exposes no local maximum-packet/intake option, so `maximumBufferedReadBytes` cannot remain an independent hard bound.
- VERIFICATION: The focused existing fork test `swift test --filter windowAdjustmentEvent` passes and confirms the current 16 KiB pipeline-delivery → 16 KiB adjustment semantic. No product code changed; adapter/root/Apple builds were not run because no conforming production transport can yet be constructed.
- ROUTING: Recommend an accepted candidate-neutral connected-socket/engine-bootstrap seam plus ReluxNIOSSH consumer-driven receive-credit and maximum-packet/intake hooks, each with deterministic tests. Exact evidence, rejected workarounds, alternatives, and resume condition are attached as `TASK-260715-1af33i_adapter-api-blocker.md`.

### 1015 — Review: fork adapter-conformance seams accepted; three adapter-facing notes (TASK-260720-34d4du)
- MILESTONE: Fork extension accepted → `done`. Reviewer independently re-ran every gate: fork-tool exact-delta verify (20-file allowlist, archive/license SHA-256), 323 upstream XCTest + 13 fork Swift Testing cases, strict `swift-format lint --strict` on all 9 changed files, `make validate-core` (61 root tests, boundary check, build) — all exit 0. Attached patch artifact SHA-256 matches working tree (`b162e69a…88cf5c`).
